<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What leaves the device, for Android 12 and up. Everything below API 31 reads backup_rules.xml
    instead, and this app ships to 24, so the two files exist and must be kept saying the same thing.

    Everything the player owns is backed up, and everything the player owns is a shared preference:
    the coins, the level they are on, the daily history and streak, and the language they chose. A
    new phone that arrives without those is a new phone that has lost the game, and there is no
    account to restore it from - the game deliberately stores nothing on a server.

    Listed one by one rather than backed up wholesale by leaving this file empty. An empty rule set
    means "take everything", which is the same instruction whether or not anybody thought about it,
    and it silently adopts every preference file added from here on. Naming them is what makes the
    next file a decision.

    ## ravel-ads, which is here on purpose and is the interesting one

    It holds today's date and how many rewarded ads have been watched against it. Including it is
    what makes a restore honest in both directions:

    - Restored on the same day the backup was taken: the watches already spent that day come back
      with it, so reinstalling is not a second three.
    - Restored later: the stored day is in the past, which reads as a fresh allowance, which is
      correct - it is a different day.
    - Restored onto a device whose clock is behind the stored day: nothing today. This is the
      branch that answers winding the date back to farm ads, and it is a rule about the *stored*
      day rather than a lockout flag, so a clock put right is forgiven immediately.

    See AdAllowance.remainingFrom, which is where all three of those actually live.

    ## The one thing this cannot defend, and when to come back

    A restored balance is a restored balance. Once coins can be bought with real money, "spend, then
    restore an older backup" duplicates them, and no rule in this file can see that happening - the
    only real answer is a balance the server owns, which means an account. Today there is nothing to
    defraud but yourself, so ravel-wallet is backed up. **The commit that adds in-app purchases is
    the commit that has to revisit this file.**
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-wallet.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-levels.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-daily.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-settings.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-ads.xml" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <!--
        Device-to-device transfer, which is a different path with the same answer. It is a copy onto
        a phone the player is holding in their other hand, so there is even less reason to hold
        anything back than there is for a cloud backup - and a transfer that dropped the ad counter
        would be a way to get three more by migrating.
    -->
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-wallet.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-levels.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-daily.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-settings.xml" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="ravel-ads.xml" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
